.TH DNET2D 8 "September 2026" "dnet2-bpf" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
dnet2d \- transport an IPv4 virtual network over two Ethernet LANs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dnet2d
.RB [ \-r " rx-object" ]
.RB [ \-t " tx-object" ]
.RB [ \-v " tap-name" ]
.RB [ \-a " address/prefix" ]
.I IFACE_A IFACE_B
.br
.B dnet2d \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dnet2d
runs in the foreground and provides two alternative prototype data paths.
The two physical interfaces must exist and be distinct.
Do not select the management or SSH interface.
.SS Virtual adapter mode
Either
.B \-v
or
.B \-a
enables TAP mode.
The daemon creates a nonpersistent Ethernet TAP adapter with default IPv4
address 172.23.1.1/16.
Applications reach the overlay through the adapter's connected route.
For the intended topology, interface A is on 172.21.* and interface B is on
172.22.*; their addresses and routes are not changed.
Both physical links must already be up.
.PP
Outbound IPv4 frames with a 172.23.* source and Ethernet/IPv4 ARP frames
targeting 172.23.* are sent unchanged on both links.
Incoming IPv4 frames targeting 172.23.*, and IPv4 broadcast or multicast
frames from 172.23.* sources, are eligible for delivery to the TAP.
Incoming ARP must target 172.23.*.
Incoming frames must also be addressed to the TAP MAC, broadcast, or multicast.
Locally transmitted frames and reflected frames with the TAP source MAC are
ignored.
There is no NAT or IP encapsulation.
.PP
Use normal Linux tools such as
.BR ip (8)
to configure the adapter while the daemon runs.
The transport filter remains 172.23.0.0/16 after address changes.
Initial MTU is the smaller physical MTU, capped at 1500.
Keep subsequent MTU changes within both physical links' limits.
The daemon refuses to adopt an existing adapter.
The kernel removes the TAP when the daemon closes it or exits, including
on a crash; runtime configuration is therefore not persistent.
.PP
TAP mode does not load or attach BPF programs.
Stop any old daemon and remove stale legacy BPF attachments before switching
modes.
Packet sockets request promiscuous reception on A and B for their lifetime.
Switches and hypervisors must allow the TAP MAC on those links.
.SS Legacy BPF mode
Without
.B \-v
or
.BR \-a ,
the daemon attaches generic/SKB XDP counters and TC egress classifiers to A
and B.
XDP passes all received traffic.
TC clones every outbound Ethernet frame to the other interface, including
ARP and IPv6, using a temporary packet mark to prevent clone recursion.
Counters are printed every two seconds.
Existing XDP programs are not replaced.
Existing clsact qdiscs are preserved.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-v " tap-name"
Enable TAP mode with the given adapter name.
The name must be unused, shorter than IFNAMSIZ, and contain no percent sign.
.TP
.BI \-a " address/prefix"
Set the TAP startup IPv4 address.
The address must be a host address in 172.23.0.0/16 with prefix length
16 through 30; network and broadcast addresses are rejected.
Default: 172.23.1.1/16.
This option also enables TAP mode, using the name
.B dnet2
unless overridden by
.BR \-v .
Use a unique address on each host.
.TP
.BI \-r " rx-object"
Legacy mode RX BPF object.
Default: /usr/local/lib/dnet2-bpf/dnet2_rx.bpf.o.
Ignored in TAP mode.
.TP
.BI \-t " tx-object"
Legacy mode TX BPF object.
Default: /usr/local/lib/dnet2-bpf/dnet2_tx.bpf.o.
Ignored in TAP mode.
.TP
.B \-h
Print usage and exit successfully.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The daemon reads command-line arguments, not a configuration file.
The supplied systemd service reads
.I /etc/dnet2/dnet2.conf
and passes its settings as arguments; see
.BR dnet2.conf (5).
The shipped service configuration enables TAP mode with name
.B dnet2
and address 172.23.1.1/16.
.SH PRIVILEGES
TAP mode requires /dev/net/tun, CAP_NET_ADMIN, and CAP_NET_RAW.
Legacy BPF mode requires privileges to load BPF programs and attach XDP/TC
hooks; the supplied service grants CAP_BPF, CAP_PERFMON, CAP_NET_ADMIN,
and CAP_SYS_ADMIN as well as CAP_NET_RAW.
Running manually as root is supported.
.SH SIGNALS
SIGINT and SIGTERM request shutdown.
TAP mode closes its sockets and adapter.
Legacy mode detaches its XDP programs if still current, removes its TC
filters, and removes clsact qdiscs it created.
A legacy-mode crash can leave attachments behind.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal shutdown or help requested.
.TP
.B 1
Startup or runtime failure, including invalid TAP configuration.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line syntax, missing arguments, or invalid or identical
physical interfaces.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create the default-address virtual adapter:
.PP
.nf
sudo ./build/dnet2d \-v dnet2 ens19 ens20
.fi
.PP
Use another host address:
.PP
.nf
sudo ./build/dnet2d \-v dnet2 \-a 172.23.1.2/16 ens19 ens20
ip addr show dev dnet2
ping \-I dnet2 172.23.1.1
.fi
.PP
Run legacy BPF mode using locally built objects:
.PP
.nf
sudo ./build/dnet2d \-r build/dnet2_rx.bpf.o \e
    \-t build/dnet2_tx.bpf.o ens19 ens20
.fi
.PP
Inspect overlay traffic:
.PP
.nf
sudo tcpdump \-eni ens19 'arp or net 172.23.0.0/16'
sudo tcpdump \-eni ens20 'arp or net 172.23.0.0/16'
.fi
.SH RECOVERY
After a legacy-mode crash, verify the interface names and ownership of
attachments before removing them:
.PP
.nf
sudo ip link set dev ens19 xdpgeneric off
sudo ip link set dev ens20 xdpgeneric off
sudo tc filter del dev ens19 egress protocol all pref 1 handle 1 bpf
sudo tc filter del dev ens20 egress protocol all pref 1 handle 1 bpf
.fi
.PP
Do not delete clsact blindly; inspect both ingress and egress filters first.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Windows DNET2 wire compatibility is unverified.
DNET2 sequence headers and RX duplicate suppression are not implemented.
Packets arriving on both LANs may be delivered twice, including UDP.
TAP mode does not transport IPv6 or VLAN-tagged frames; use VLAN
subinterfaces for A/B when needed.
.PP
Linux may answer ARP for a local address on other interfaces.
If necessary, configure arp_ignore=1 on A/B through the normal sysctl setup.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/dnet2/dnet2.conf
Settings for the supplied systemd service.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/dnet2.service
Installed service unit.
.TP
.I /dev/net/tun
TUN/TAP device used in virtual adapter mode.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dnet2.conf (5),
.BR ip (8),
.BR tc (8),
.BR tcpdump (8),
.BR systemctl (1)
